      "what_must_be_true": "The AI datacenter build-out sustains worldwide WFE growth through at least 2027; customers' eight-quarter rolling forecasts convert into shipments roughly on schedule (DRAM/HBM and gate-all-around leading-edge logic in particular); value-based pricing holds company gross margin at or above 50%; the doubled manufacturing footprint built through 2028 is absorbed by demand rather than stranded as oversupply; and export-control rules do not remove China demand faster than leading-edge ramps can replace it."
    },
    "preview_note": "AMAT's Q3 FY26 print is already in the filed record: record revenue of $9.115B (+25% YoY), GAAP EPS $3.17, record non-GAAP EPS $3.50, record CFO of $3.04B and FCF of $2.33B. The debate now shifts to the November 12 Q4 print. Three factors decide the reaction: (1) whether Semiconductor Systems lands at or above the ~$7.9B guide, with management flagging a 'very significant' DRAM ramp into H2 CY26 as customers unlock cleanroom-space workarounds; (2) gross margin durability at the guided ~50.4% non-GAAP level as value pricing offsets input costs; (3) the FY27 setup - a 14-week Q1, opex step-up, ~13% tax rate, and whether the eight-quarter customer visibility converts to another upward revision. With $12.8B of buyback authorization and 80-100% FCF payout, capital return is the floor under the story; any wobble in systems momentum or margin is the ceiling.",
    "reasoning": "As of today (2026-08-20) the Q3 FY2026 results are no longer unknown: AMAT filed them in an 8-K on 2026-08-13, and the IR earnings deck/prepared remarks are archived as IR-OBSERVED pages. I therefore anchor the quarter on the filed actuals rather than inventing an estimate: revenue $9,115M, GAAP net income $2,538M, non-GAAP net income $2,795M (bridge adjustments of +257 as filed), CFO $3,037M, capex $707M, ending cash+restricted $7,094M, total assets $43,522M. GAAP diluted EPS is 2538/800 = 3.17; headline adjusted EPS is computed as 2795/800 = 3.49 (the company prints $3.50 on unrounded figures). For Q4 FY26 I take guidance ($10.25B/$4.02) with a slight upward bias to $10.3B given record sequential momentum, 'highest QoQ growth in company history' in Q3 and repeated upward revisions through the year. FY2027 builds on the company's own 'strong, record year' framing: 14-week Q1, DRAM and GAA ramps, packaging >70% CY26 growth, AGS >20%, ~13% tax; FY2028-29 normalize to low-double-digit WFE growth as the capacity doubling is absorbed. Balance sheets roll from filed actuals; buybacks step up toward the 80-100% FCF payout commitment. WFE levels are my own estimates; segment decompositions tie to the filed segment tables."
  },
